	MERGING


# you are in your main branch an decide you want to work on SPI so you do:
git checkout -b SPI
# you do some work and do some commits, doing so moves the SPI forward
# because you have it checked out (that is, your HEAD is pointing to it)
# suddenly issues emerge from the LED display (GPIO), so you switch back
# to your main branch. After commiting SPI. If you don't commit you can
# get around this with stashing and commit amending.
git checkout main
# At this point, your project working directory is exactly 
# the way it was before you started working on SPI.
git checkout -b GPIO
# you fix the issue and commit it
git checkout main
git merge GPIO
# after you fixd the issue with LED display, you are ready to continue with SPI,
# However, first you’ll delete the GPIO branch, because you no longer
# need it — the main branch points at the same place
git branch -d GPIO
# Now you can switch back to your work-in-progress branch SPI
git checkout SPI

# It’s worth noting here that the work you did in your GPIO branch is not contained 
# in the files in your SPI branch. If you need to pull it in, you can merge your main
# branch into your SPI branch by running git merge main, or you can wait to integrate
# those changes until you decide to pull the SPI branch back into main later.
# When your SPI is completed just do:
git checkout main
git merge SPI
git branch -d SPI



